.policy "alembic-practice-column-type-rewrite" revision 1
.description "Move an existing column to a new type through a new column and a backfill, or add a CHECK constraint for a length limit, so PostgreSQL does not rewrite the table under an exclusive lock."
.language python
.mode steer
.scope include "**/*.py"
// Rerail's own rule, not a port. `ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN c TYPE new` takes an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock (no reads or
// writes) and "will normally cause the entire table and its indexes to be rewritten", unless the old type is binary
// coercible to the new one (PostgreSQL, ALTER TABLE notes). Shrinking or adding a length limit is no binary coercion
// either: PostgreSQL re-reads every row against the limit under the same lock.
// Reported: in `upgrade()` (or a module-level helper it calls), `op.alter_column(t, c, type_=NEW, ...)` or
// `batch_op.alter_column(c, type_=NEW, ...)` on a table that upgrade() does not create, judged against
// `existing_type=OLD`:
//   op.alter_column("runs", "tokens", existing_type=sa.INTEGER(), type_=sa.BigInteger())          reported
//   op.alter_column("docs", "meta", existing_type=postgresql.JSON(), type_=postgresql.JSONB())     reported
//   op.alter_column("tools", "name", existing_type=sa.VARCHAR(256), type_=sa.String(255))        reported (limit)
//   op.alter_column("user", "email", existing_type=sa.String(), type_=sa.String(255))            reported (limit)
//   op.alter_column("tools", "name", existing_type=sa.VARCHAR(40), type_=sa.String(255))         not reported
// Left alone, as PostgreSQL makes them without touching the rows: VARCHAR/String/AutoString(n) to a length m >= n, to
// an unlimited String() or to Text/UnicodeText; Text to String() without a length; Numeric(p, s) to Numeric(p2, s) with
// p2 >= p, or to an unconstrained Numeric(); LargeBinary to LargeBinary (both BYTEA); the same type text on both sides;
// the same PostgreSQL type spelled two ways (TIMESTAMP and DateTime without a time zone, either with one; UUID and
// Uuid; INTEGER and Integer; BIGINT and BigInteger; BOOLEAN and Boolean; TEXT and Text; JSON, JSONB, DATE), which
// autogenerate restates next to a nullability change.
// Types are read by the class they resolve to (lib/alembic's al_type_class_t and al_pg_type_t), through imports and
// aliases, a project's TypeDecorator (`impl = TEXT`), a subclass of a SQLAlchemy type, and a project function that
// returns a type (superset's `MediumText()`, `Text().with_variant(MEDIUMTEXT(), "mysql")`, is TEXT on PostgreSQL); a
// target from `sqlalchemy.dialects.mysql` (`mysql.LONGTEXT`) is MySQL-only. A type of another module is that module's
// answer, by symbol: the rule reads each call and type expression by its term (al_term), and the finding reads the
// call back by its term. Also left alone: a length that is no integer literal (`sa.String(current_length)`) where it
// decides; an
// `existing_type` that is no type expression (a conditional); a call without `existing_type` whose target is a
// string, text or numeric type (it may be a widening; the rule cannot tell); a table the same upgrade() creates; the
// branch of a dialect test that runs only on another database; a call in an `except` handler (superset's non-Postgres
// fallback after a `try:` that already made the change); downgrade(); tests; an `op` that is not `alembic.op`.
// Gaps: types are judged by their names (the last part of the callee), not resolved; a table named by a variable is
// not seen; `DateTime()` to `DateTime(timezone=True)` is reported although PostgreSQL 12+ skips the rewrite when the
// session time zone is UTC; a `postgresql_using=` that is a plain relabel of a widening is left alone with it; an array
// of a widened VARCHAR is reported; raw `op.execute("ALTER TABLE ... TYPE ...")` is not read.
// Corpus (8 Alembic repositories): 34 matches, all read, all real type changes; 32 actionable (mealie migrates before
// it serves). See the README's rule details.

#include "lib/alembic.rerail"

.violation TypeChangeRewrites(at: node, col: symbol, table: symbol, change: symbol) "Changing `{col}` of existing `{table}` {change} rewrites the table under an exclusive lock. Add a new column, backfill it in batches, switch to it, then drop the old one. Keep it for a small table."
.violation LengthLimitScan(at: node, col: symbol, table: symbol, change: symbol, limit: number) "Changing `{col}` of existing `{table}` {change} checks every row under an exclusive lock. Keep the type and add a NOT VALID length CHECK, validated in a later revision. Keep it for a small table."

// ---- type expressions --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Each type expression of an alter_column, by its document d and term et (al_term), with its own file's syntax:
// ct_arg_at(d, et), it is alter_column's type_ or existing_type; ct_text_at(d, et, s), its text; ct_call_at(d, et), it
// is a call; ct_len_arg_at, ct_scale_arg_at, ct_any_arg_at: it passes a length, a scale, any argument.
.decl ct_arg(e: node)
ct_arg(e) :- al_kw(c, "type_", v), al_call(c, "alter_column", _), unparen(v, e).
ct_arg(e) :- al_kw(c, "existing_type", v), al_call(c, "alter_column", _), unparen(v, e).
.decl ct_arg_at(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_arg_at(d, et) :- ct_arg(e), al_term(e, d, et).
.decl ct_text_at(d: number, et: symbol, s: symbol)
ct_text_at(d, et, s) :- ct_arg(e), text(e, s), al_term(e, d, et).
.decl ct_call_at(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_call_at(d, et) :- ct_arg(e), kind(e, "call"), al_term(e, d, et).
.decl ct_len_arg_at(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_len_arg_at(d, et) :- ct_arg(e), (call_arg(e, 0, "", _) ; call_arg(e, -1, "length", _)), al_term(e, d, et).
.decl ct_scale_arg_at(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_scale_arg_at(d, et) :- ct_arg(e), (call_arg(e, 1, "", _) ; call_arg(e, -1, "scale", _)), al_term(e, d, et).
.decl ct_any_arg_at(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_any_arg_at(d, et) :- ct_arg(e), call_arg(e, _, _, _), al_term(e, d, et).
// ct_int_at(d, et, i, k, v): call et passes the integer literal v positionally at i or as keyword k (the keyword of
// position i).
.decl ct_int_slot(i: number, k: symbol)
ct_int_slot(0, "length"). ct_int_slot(0, "precision"). ct_int_slot(1, "scale").
.decl ct_int_at(d: number, et: symbol, i: number, k: symbol, v: number)
ct_int_at(d, et, i, k, v) :- ct_arg(e), ct_int_slot(i, k), (call_arg(e, i, "", a) ; call_arg(e, -1, k, a)),
    unparen(a, u), kind(u, "integer"), text(u, s), v = to_number(s), al_term(e, d, et).
// ct_name(d, et, n): the class type et resolves to, lowercased (lib/alembic's al_type_class_t).
.decl ct_name(d: number, et: symbol, n: symbol)
ct_name(d, et, n) :- ct_arg_at(d, et), al_type_class_t(d, et, n).
.decl ct_mysql(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_mysql(d, et) :- ct_arg_at(d, et), al_mysql_type_t(d, et).
// The families PostgreSQL treats alike for a type change.
.decl ct_string(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_string(d, et) :- ct_arg_at(d, et), al_pg_type_t(d, et, "varchar").
.decl ct_text(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_text(d, et) :- ct_arg_at(d, et), al_pg_type_t(d, et, "text").
.decl ct_numeric(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_numeric(d, et) :- ct_arg_at(d, et), al_pg_type_t(d, et, "numeric").
.decl ct_binary(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_binary(d, et) :- ct_arg_at(d, et), al_pg_type_t(d, et, "bytea").
// ct_canon(d, et, k): the PostgreSQL type k that et renders, when k carries no parameter the rule must compare (so
// `sa.TIMESTAMP()` and `sa.DateTime()`, `postgresql.UUID()` and `sa.Uuid()`, `sa.INTEGER()` and `sa.Integer()` are one).
.decl ct_canon(d: number, et: symbol, k: symbol)
ct_canon(d, et, k) :- ct_arg_at(d, et), al_pg_type_t(d, et, k), k != "varchar", k != "numeric", k != "char".

// String lengths: a literal, none (unlimited VARCHAR), or something else (unknown).
.decl ct_len(d: number, et: symbol, n: number)
ct_len(d, et, n) :- ct_string(d, et), ct_int_at(d, et, 0, "length", n).
.decl ct_unlimited(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_unlimited(d, et) :- ct_string(d, et), !ct_call_at(d, et).
ct_unlimited(d, et) :- ct_string(d, et), ct_call_at(d, et), !ct_len_arg_at(d, et).
.decl ct_len_unknown(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_len_unknown(d, et) :- ct_string(d, et), !ct_len(d, et, _), !ct_unlimited(d, et).
// Numeric precision and scale (scale 0 when only a precision is given); unconstrained: no precision.
.decl ct_num(d: number, et: symbol, p: number, s: number)
ct_num(d, et, p, s) :- ct_numeric(d, et), ct_int_at(d, et, 0, "precision", p), ct_int_at(d, et, 1, "scale", s).
ct_num(d, et, p, 0) :- ct_numeric(d, et), ct_int_at(d, et, 0, "precision", p), !ct_scale_arg_at(d, et).
.decl ct_num_unconstrained(d: number, et: symbol)
ct_num_unconstrained(d, et) :- ct_numeric(d, et), !ct_call_at(d, et).
ct_num_unconstrained(d, et) :- ct_numeric(d, et), ct_call_at(d, et), !ct_any_arg_at(d, et).

// ---- the change ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ct_change_at(d, ct, x, t, nt): alter_column ct of revision d changes column x of existing table t to type nt,
// outside a non-PostgreSQL branch of its own file, an except handler and test code; ct_old_at(d, ct, ot): its
// existing_type. ct_change: no test function of another module rules PostgreSQL out (al_np_ext).
.decl ct_change_at(d: number, ct: symbol, x: symbol, t: symbol, nt: symbol)
ct_change_at(d, ct, x, t, nt) :- al_on_existing(c, "alter_column", t, _), al_kw(c, "type_", v), unparen(v, n),
    al_role(c, "column_name", e), al_name_value(e, x), !al_non_pg(c), !ct_in_except(c), docof(c, d),
    al_migration_doc(d), !al_test_doc(d), al_term(c, d, ct), al_term(n, d, nt).
.decl ct_in_except(c: node)
ct_in_except(c) :- al_call(c, "alter_column", _), kind(h, "except_clause"), within(c, h).
.decl ct_old_at(d: number, ct: symbol, ot: symbol)
ct_old_at(d, ct, ot) :- al_call(c, "alter_column", _), al_kw(c, "existing_type", v), unparen(v, o), al_term(c, d, ct),
    al_term(o, d, ot).
.decl ct_change(d: number, ct: symbol, x: symbol, t: symbol, nt: symbol)
ct_change(d, ct, x, t, nt) :- ct_change_at(d, ct, x, t, nt), !al_np_ext(d, ct).
.decl ct_old(d: number, ct: symbol, ot: symbol)
ct_old(d, ct, ot) :- ct_change(d, ct, _, _, _), ct_old_at(d, ct, ot).
.decl ct_has_old(d: number, ct: symbol)
ct_has_old(d, ct) :- ct_old(d, ct, _).

// ct_safe(d, ct): PostgreSQL changes call ct's type ot to nt in the catalog only (binary coercible, or a widened limit).
.decl ct_safe(d: number, ct: symbol)
ct_safe(d, ct) :- ct_old(d, ct, ot), ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_text_at(d, ot, s), ct_text_at(d, nt, s).
ct_safe(d, ct) :- ct_old(d, ct, ot), ct_canon(d, ot, k), ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_canon(d, nt, k).
ct_safe(d, ct) :- ct_old(d, ct, ot), ct_len(d, ot, a), ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_len(d, nt, b), b >= a.
ct_safe(d, ct) :- ct_old(d, ct, ot), ct_string(d, ot), ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_unlimited(d, nt).
ct_safe(d, ct) :- ct_old(d, ct, ot), (ct_string(d, ot) ; ct_text(d, ot)), ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_text(d, nt).
ct_safe(d, ct) :- ct_old(d, ct, ot), ct_text(d, ot), ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_unlimited(d, nt).
ct_safe(d, ct) :- ct_old(d, ct, ot), ct_num(d, ot, p, s), ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_num(d, nt, p2, s), p2 >= p.
ct_safe(d, ct) :- ct_old(d, ct, ot), ct_numeric(d, ot), ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_num_unconstrained(d, nt).
ct_safe(d, ct) :- ct_old(d, ct, ot), ct_binary(d, ot), ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_binary(d, nt).

// ct_unknown(d, ct): the rule cannot judge the change: a MySQL-only target, a length that is no literal where it
// decides, an existing_type that is no type expression, or no existing_type and a target that may widen.
.decl ct_unknown(d: number, ct: symbol)
ct_unknown(d, ct) :- ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_mysql(d, nt).
ct_unknown(d, ct) :- ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_len_unknown(d, nt).
ct_unknown(d, ct) :- ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_len(d, nt, _), ct_old(d, ct, ot), ct_len_unknown(d, ot).
ct_unknown(d, ct) :- ct_old(d, ct, ot), !ct_name(d, ot, _).
ct_unknown(d, ct) :- ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), !ct_has_old(d, ct), (ct_string(d, nt) ; ct_text(d, nt) ;
    ct_numeric(d, nt)).

.decl ct_hit(d: number, ct: symbol, x: symbol, t: symbol, change: symbol)
ct_hit(d, ct, x, t, ch) :- ct_change(d, ct, x, t, nt), ct_old(d, ct, ot), !ct_safe(d, ct), !ct_unknown(d, ct),
    ct_text_at(d, ot, so), ct_text_at(d, nt, sn), ch = cat(cat(cat(cat("from `", so), "` to `"), sn), "`").
ct_hit(d, ct, x, t, ch) :- ct_change(d, ct, x, t, nt), !ct_has_old(d, ct), !ct_unknown(d, ct), ct_text_at(d, nt, sn),
    ch = cat(cat("to `", sn), "`").

// A length limit: a literal-length VARCHAR target from a string or text type.
.decl ct_limit(d: number, ct: symbol, m: number)
ct_limit(d, ct, m) :- ct_change(d, ct, _, _, nt), ct_len(d, nt, m), ct_old(d, ct, ot), (ct_string(d, ot) ;
    ct_text(d, ot)).

// ct_call(c, d, ct): alter_column c of document d has the term ct, by which a finding reads its answers.
.decl ct_call(c: node, d: number, ct: symbol)
ct_call(c, d, ct) :- al_call(c, "alter_column", _), al_term(c, d, ct).

TypeChangeRewrites(c, x, t, ch) :- ct_call(c, d, ct), ct_hit(d, ct, x, t, ch), !ct_limit(d, ct, _).
LengthLimitScan(c, x, t, ch, m) :- ct_call(c, d, ct), ct_hit(d, ct, x, t, ch), ct_limit(d, ct, m).

// The cases, `upstream/alembic-practice/authored/alembic-practice-column-type-rewrite/`, read as one repository: each
// line ending in `# caught` is reported, and no other line.
.test cases exactly
    .fixture "app/other_op.py" from "../upstream/alembic-practice/authored/alembic-practice-column-type-rewrite/app/other_op.py"
    .fixture "app/types.py" from "../upstream/alembic-practice/authored/alembic-practice-column-type-rewrite/app/types.py"
    .fixture "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py" from "../upstream/alembic-practice/authored/alembic-practice-column-type-rewrite/migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py"
    .fixture "migrations/versions/b2_dialects.py" from "../upstream/alembic-practice/authored/alembic-practice-column-type-rewrite/migrations/versions/b2_dialects.py"
    .fixture "migrations/versions/b3_spellings.py" from "../upstream/alembic-practice/authored/alembic-practice-column-type-rewrite/migrations/versions/b3_spellings.py"
    .fixture "migrations/versions/b4_project_types.py" from "../upstream/alembic-practice/authored/alembic-practice-column-type-rewrite/migrations/versions/b4_project_types.py"
    .fixture "tests/test_migration_shapes.py" from "../upstream/alembic-practice/authored/alembic-practice-column-type-rewrite/tests/test_migration_shapes.py"
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":20
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":21
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":22
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":23
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":24
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":25
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":26
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":27
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":35
    .expect LengthLimitScan at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":43
    .expect LengthLimitScan at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":44
    .expect LengthLimitScan at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":45
    .expect LengthLimitScan at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":46
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b1_type_changes.py":48
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b2_dialects.py":22
    .expect LengthLimitScan at "migrations/versions/b2_dialects.py":30
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b2_dialects.py":32
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b2_dialects.py":48
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b3_spellings.py":25
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b3_spellings.py":26
    .expect LengthLimitScan at "migrations/versions/b3_spellings.py":27
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b3_spellings.py":28
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b4_project_types.py":26
    .expect TypeChangeRewrites at "migrations/versions/b4_project_types.py":27
    .expect LengthLimitScan at "migrations/versions/b4_project_types.py":28
.end
